WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.458
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.458 --> 00:00:05.555
we help some happy honey-makers
fix their regional performance.

00:00:05.555 --> 00:00:07.180
Want to see what all
the buzz is about?

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.138
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:10.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.840
The Bee Party is a
conservation-focused

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.750
organization which provides
data scientists, researchers,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.610
farmers, and
agricultural companies

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.820
with the data they need to help
preserve the mighty honeybee.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.411
Since their efforts had
now achieved critical mass,

00:00:24.411 --> 00:00:26.410
their technology team
started getting complaints

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:28.960
from their global regions
that data access was slower

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:29.830
than desired.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.320
Now, regional performance
is a common problem.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.180
Because, you know, physics.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.020
But if the network has
been set up properly,

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.020
this shouldn't "bee" an issue.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.549
Anyhow, when we
talk about latency,

00:00:42.549 --> 00:00:44.590
one of the most important
things we must remember

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.135
is that it's a
function of physics.

00:00:46.135 --> 00:00:48.370
Now, the speed of light
traveling through a fiber

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.770
optic cable takes roughly
about 10 milliseconds

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.000
to travel about
1,000 kilometers.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.240
So the further away you
are from your destination,

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.990
the higher your latency.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.570
This is why Google
Cloud Platform

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.670
places new data centers
in various regions

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:01.820
around the world.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.750
The goal is to find the sweet
spot between distance, latency,

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.034
and disaster tolerance.

00:01:06.034 --> 00:01:07.450
To get a sense of
this behavior, I

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:09.790
set up an instance
in each GCP region

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.530
and pinged them from
my desktop machine

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.140
here in Austin, Texas
about 100 times.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.195
As expected, the farther
away the instance

00:01:16.195 --> 00:01:17.950
is, the worse the latency to it.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.270
But I'm just one data point.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:20.936
Probably more important
is to understand

00:01:20.936 --> 00:01:23.300
the performance
between each region,

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.730
which is
straightforward to test.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.904
We already had the instances
set up in each region,

00:01:26.904 --> 00:01:29.320
so we just had to measure the
throughput from one location

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.940
to each other location.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:32.770
From this graph,
we can clearly see

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.770
that the Europe West to
Asia East and the US West

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.790
to Asia East are some of the
lowest throughput connections,

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.660
while any of the US
to US connections

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.505
have some of the
highest throughput.

00:01:42.505 --> 00:01:44.380
Now that we have an
understanding of baseline

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.880
performance, let's
try to figure out

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.830
how the bee party is set up.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.680
And as always, I start
with logging data.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.237
Looking at the user
agents fields of our logs,

00:01:53.237 --> 00:01:54.820
we can see that most
of their requests

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.387
are coming from
desktop connections.

00:01:56.387 --> 00:01:57.970
This is good, since
it means we're not

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.240
getting skewed results due to
2G or 3G connection speeds.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.590
No worries there.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.320
When we drill in some, we
can see the slower traces,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.370
we can start to see
a consistent pattern.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.699
Most of the highest
density and latency traces

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:11.800
are coming from
areas in Japan this

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.960
threw up a big red
flag for me, since I

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.600
knew that the bee party was a
nonprofit company stationed out

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:17.410
of Europe.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.930
A quick phone call with
their CTO confirmed this.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.670
The Bee Party is
based out of Germany,

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:24.100
and they deploy all
their GCE instances

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.390
in the European region.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.220
Well of course,
there's our problem.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.650
The majority of their clients
are coming from Japan,

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.032
while the instances are
only deployed in Europe.

00:02:32.032 --> 00:02:34.240
From our previous graph, we
saw that this was already

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.220
one of the lowest
performing connections.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.890
The solution for The Bee
Party was quite clear.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.350
They needed to play instances
of their VMs in regions

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.840
closer to their primary
user base, which requires

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.680
us to change two things.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.130
Firstly is creating a new
instance group for the Asia

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.470
East region so the
researchers in Japan

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.270
can be connecting to the
closest instances possible.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:56.230
Secondly is putting together
a global HTTP load balancer

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.690
whose job is to route
requests to the nearest

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:00.010
healthy instances.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.080
This way, clients in
Japan get their packets

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.120
routed to the Asia
East cluster rather

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.490
than having to go all
the way to Europe for it.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.500
Oh, by the way, did
you know that Japan

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.170
has one of the most awesome
type of honeybee on the planet?

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.480
The Japanese honeybee
has a unique defense

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.370
for fighting predatory wasps.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.850
They swarm it, hug it, and
start vibrating fast enough

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:21.550
to raise the internal
heat of their bee ball

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.550
to 117 degree Fahrenheit,
killing the hornet,

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.460
but leaving the bees unharmed,
which you've got to admit

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:28.807
is pretty cool.

00:03:28.807 --> 00:03:31.390
Anyhow, if you'd like to improve
the performance of your cloud

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.014
applications, don't
forget to check out

00:03:33.014 --> 00:03:35.350
the rest of our "Cloud
Performance Atlas" content.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.630
And when it comes to the
environment, remember,

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:38.980
every honeybee counts.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

